## 6. Conclusion

This dissertation has presented the complete theoretical architecture of the Planetary Intelligence Observatory: a hierarchical probabilistic inference engine for real-time civilizational risk synthesis. The four principal contributions — the Hierarchical Dawid-Skene source reliability layer, the formal Bayesian surprise novelty detector, the GFlowNet structural learning layer, and the second-order aleatoric-epistemic uncertainty decomposition — collectively address the three structural failures of existing risk monitoring: lack of probabilistic grounding, failure of uncertainty communication, and inability to detect structural surprises.

The system is designed from the ground up to be transparent, forkable, and resistant to false precision. Its primary value is not prediction but *observability*: making the structure of our collective ignorance about civilizational-scale risk visible, decomposable, and open to continuous revision as the world changes.

The technical challenges are substantial, but none are in principle insurmountable with currently existing tools. The remaining theoretical open problems — non-parametric structural learning, cross-domain causal identification, strategic equilibrium analysis, and calibration under uniqueness — define the frontier of this research program and constitute a rich agenda for future work.

If successful, the PIO could perform for civilizational risk what weather forecasting did for meteorology: transform a domain of guesswork and fragmented intuition into a continuous, transparent, and genuinely useful piece of public epistemic infrastructure. In an era where planetary-scale threats are accelerating while our collective sense-making apparatus remains dangerously fragmented, the construction of such infrastructure is not merely a theoretical exercise. It is an urgent practical necessity.

Appendix A Detailed Proofs for the Reliability Layer

A.1 Identifiability of the Hierarchical Dawid-Skene Model (Proof of Proposition 2.1)

We restate the model and the identifiability claim. Consider a single indicator $i$ with $L$ discrete true states $H^*_{i,t} \in \{1,\dots,L\}$. For each source $s \in \mathcal{S}_i$, the confusion matrix is $\mathbf{\Pi}^{(s,i)}$ with entries $\pi^{(s,i)}_{kl} = \Pr(O_{i,t}^{(s)} = l \mid H^*_{i,t} = k)$. The observed data are the label assignments from $|\mathcal{S}_i|$ sources across time steps. The hierarchical prior (Definition 2.9) imposes that the logits $\gamma^{(s,i)}_{k,k'}$ are drawn from a population distribution $\mathcal{N}(\mu^{(i)}_{k,k'}, \sigma^{(i)}_{k,k'})$; then $\pi^{(s,i)}_{k,\cdot} = \operatorname{softmax}(\gamma^{(s,i)}_{k,\cdot})$. The population-level parameters $(\mu,\sigma)$ have their own hyperpriors.

The claim: Under conditions (i) $|\mathcal{S}_i| \ge 3$, (ii) each $\mathbf{\Pi}^{(s,i)}$ is diagonally dominant ($\pi^{(s,i)}_{kk} > 1/L$ for all $k$), and (iii) the true label distribution $P(H^*_{i,t}=k)$ is non-degenerate, the model parameters $\{\mathbf{\Pi}^{(s,i)}\}$ and the label distribution are identifiable up to label permutation.

Proof. We adapt the spectral method of Zhang et al. (2014) to the hierarchical case. For a fixed time $t$, let $\mathbf{O}_t = (O^{(1)}_{i,t},\dots,O^{(|\mathcal{S}_i|)}_{i,t})$ be the vector of observations from all sources. The joint distribution of three sources $a,b,c$ can be expressed via the third-order moment tensor. For any three distinct sources, the probability of observing labels $(l_a,l_b,l_c)$ is

\Pr(O^{(a)}=l_a, O^{(b)}=l_b, O^{(c)}=l_c) = \sum_{k=1}^L \Pr(H^*=k) \, \pi^{(a)}_{k l_a} \pi^{(b)}_{k l_b} \pi^{(c)}_{k l_c}.

This can be written as a tensor of size $L \times L \times L$. Using the standard spectral decomposition for three-view mixture models (Anandkumar et al., 2014), if the confusion matrices are full column rank and the label distribution has full support, the tensors uniquely identify the confusion matrices and the label distribution up to a permutation of the latent states.

Now, the diagonal dominance condition $\pi^{(s)}_{kk} > 1/L$ ensures that each confusion matrix is not a uniform matrix and, together with the fact that the matrices are stochastic, implies that they are non-singular (full rank). In fact, a diagonally dominant stochastic matrix with positive diagonal entries is invertible (Horn and Johnson, 1991, Cor. 6.2.9). Since we have at least three sources, the third-order moment tensor suffices to recover the parameters up to permutation. The hierarchical prior does not affect identifiability: the population parameters merely govern the distribution of the confusion matrices, but the observed tensor still factorizes as above with the true $\Pi^{(s)}$ for each source. The only additional condition needed is that the sources are conditionally independent given the true label, which is part of the model.

Therefore, under the stated conditions, the confusion matrices $\mathbf{\Pi}^{(s,i)}$ and the label marginals are identifiable up to relabeling of the latent states. ∎

Remark: In the HDS model, the hierarchical prior provides shrinkage; identifiability is not compromised because the hyperparameters are inferred from the population of sources and do not impose a degenerate structure.

A.2 Convergence of Online Reliability Estimation (Proof of Proposition 2.2)

We analyze the stochastic variational inference (SVI) algorithm for updating the posterior over the HDS parameters $(\mathbf{\Pi},\boldsymbol{\mu},\boldsymbol{\sigma})$. At each time step $t$, we receive a batch of observations $\mathbf{O}_t$ and perform a single gradient step on the evidence lower bound (ELBO).

Let $\Theta$ denote all variational parameters (e.g., for a mean-field approximation, the variational parameters of the Dirichlet posteriors over rows of $\Pi^{(s)}$, and Gaussian parameters for $\mu,\sigma$). The ELBO is

\mathcal{L}(\Theta) = \mathbb{E}_{q_\Theta}[\log P(\mathbf{O}_t, \Theta)] + H(q_\Theta),

and its gradient with respect to $\Theta$ is estimated via the score function or reparameterization trick. Under standard Robbins–Monro conditions (step sizes $\eta_t$ satisfying $\sum \eta_t = \infty$, $\sum \eta_t^2 < \infty$), and if the gradient estimator has bounded variance, the SVI procedure converges to a local optimum of the ELBO.

We now prove the stated bound $\mathbb{E}[\|\hat{g}_t - \nabla \mathcal{L}(\Theta_t)\|^2] \le C/t$, which implies $\|\Theta_t - \Theta^*\|^2 = O(1/t)$ under strong convexity of the negative ELBO near the optimum.

Proof. The model uses the hierarchical Dawid–Skene specification; the ELBO is non-convex but we assume the algorithm has entered a basin where it is locally strongly convex (a common assumption for asymptotic analysis of SVI). Let $\Theta^*$ be the unique maximizer of $\mathcal{L}$ in that region. The stochastic gradient at step $t$ using a minibatch of size $B$ can be written as $\hat{g}_t = \frac{1}{B} \sum_{b=1}^B \nabla \log p(\mathbf{o}_{t,b} \mid \Theta_t) + \nabla \log p(\Theta_t) - \nabla \log q(\Theta_t)$. The variance of $\hat{g}_t$ arises from the stochasticity of the data and the score function estimator if using discrete variables.

For the hierarchical Dawid–Skene with continuous latent variables after the softmax transformation, one can use the reparameterization trick for the Gaussian variables $\gamma$, making the gradient estimator's variance bounded by a constant $V$ (Kingma and Welling, 2014). Hence $\mathbb{E}[\|\hat{g}_t - \nabla \mathcal{L}(\Theta_t)\|^2] \le V/B$. By using a decaying step size $\eta_t = \eta_0 / t$, the contribution of the variance to the mean squared error after $t$ steps is bounded by $\eta_0^2 V/(B t)$ times a constant depending on the condition number (Bottou, 1998). Taking $C = \eta_0^2 V / B$ yields the $O(1/t)$ rate. Under the Polyak–Łojasiewicz condition, this translates to $\|\Theta_t - \Theta^*\|^2 = O(1/t)$ in expectation. ∎

Detailed derivation: Suppose the objective $\mathcal{L}(\Theta)$ is $\mu$-strongly convex in a neighborhood of $\Theta^*$. Then for any $\Theta$ in that neighborhood,

\langle \nabla \mathcal{L}(\Theta), \Theta - \Theta^* \rangle \ge \mu \|\Theta - \Theta^*\|^2.

With step size $\eta_t$, the iterate update is $\Theta_{t+1} = \Theta_t + \eta_t \hat{g}_t$. Standard SGD analysis gives

\mathbb{E}[\|\Theta_{t+1} - \Theta^*\|^2] \le (1 - 2\eta_t \mu) \mathbb{E}[\|\Theta_t - \Theta^*\|^2] + \eta_t^2 \sigma^2,

where $\sigma^2$ bounds the gradient variance. By unrolling the recursion with $\eta_t = \eta_0/t$, one obtains $\mathbb{E}[\|\Theta_t - \Theta^*\|^2] \le C' / t$ for some constant $C'$, which implies the desired bound on gradient error via the Lipschitz continuity of the gradient. Choosing $C$ appropriately yields the statement.

A.3 Robustness under Huber’s Contamination Model

We provide details on the MMD-robust posterior (Definition 2.7). For indicator $i$ with true data-generating distribution $F_i(\cdot \mid \mathbf{H})$, the observed distribution under contamination is $\mathbb{P}_i^* = (1-\varepsilon_i) F_i + \varepsilon_i Q_i$, with $Q_i$ an arbitrary contamination distribution. The classical Bayesian update using the log-likelihood $\log F_i$ can be severely biased even for small $\varepsilon_i$, especially if $Q_i$ is heavy-tailed. The MMD-Bayes framework (Cherief-Abdellatif and Alquier, 2020) replaces the log-likelihood with a robust criterion based on the maximum mean discrepancy (MMD). The posterior is defined as

P_{\text{robust}}(\mathbf{H} \mid \mathbf{O}_{1:n}) \propto P(\mathbf{H}) \exp\left(-n \cdot \operatorname{MMD}^2(\hat{\mathbb{P}}_n, F_{\mathbf{H}})\right),

where $\hat{\mathbb{P}}_n$ is the empirical distribution of the $n$ observations and $\operatorname{MMD}^2(P,Q) = \mathbb{E}_{x,x'\sim P}[k(x,x')] + \mathbb{E}_{y,y'\sim Q}[k(y,y')] - 2\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P, y\sim Q}[k(x,y)]$ for a characteristic kernel $k$.

Consistency under contamination. Under the assumption that the clean model is well-specified and the contamination fraction $\varepsilon_i < 1/2$, the MMD-robust posterior is consistent for the true parameter $\mathbf{H}^*$ (Alquier and Gerber, 2024). Specifically, for any $\delta > 0$,

\Pr(\|\mathbf{H} - \mathbf{H}^*\| > \delta \mid \mathbf{O}_{1:n}) \to 0 \quad \text{as } n \to \infty,

with rate depending on the kernel. The key property is that the MMD is robust to arbitrary contamination because it is an integral probability metric: outliers in the contamination distribution contribute bounded influence. In the PIO, the contamination fraction $\varepsilon_i$ is estimated simultaneously with the reliability parameters; the robust likelihood is used to weight observations adaptively. This robustness property ensures that the overall inference is not dominated by a few manipulated sources, complementing the HDS reliability layer.

