Appendix B Novelty Detection and Bayesian Surprise

B.1 Uniqueness Theorem (Proof of Theorem 2.1)

Theorem 2.1 (Uniqueness). Let $\mathcal{M}$ be a hypothesis space with prior $P_{\text{prior}}$ and posterior $P_{\text{post}}$ after observing data $D$. A measure $\mathcal{S}(P_{\text{post}}, P_{\text{prior}})$ of the impact of the data satisfies:

· (i) $\mathcal{S} = 0$ iff $P_{\text{post}} = P_{\text{prior}}$,
· (ii) $\mathcal{S}$ is invariant under reparameterization (i.e., for any bijective transformation $\phi$ of $\mathcal{M}$, $\mathcal{S}(P_{\text{post}}^\phi, P_{\text{prior}}^\phi) = \mathcal{S}(P_{\text{post}}, P_{\text{prior}})$ where $P^\phi$ is the pushforward measure),
· (iii) For conditionally independent data $D_1, D_2$, the total surprise decomposes additively: $\mathcal{S}(P_{\text{post}}(\cdot|D_1,D_2), P_{\text{prior}}) = \mathcal{S}(P_{\text{post}}(\cdot|D_1), P_{\text{prior}}) + \mathcal{S}(P_{\text{post}}(\cdot|D_1,D_2), P_{\text{post}}(\cdot|D_1))$.
  Then $\mathcal{S}$ must be a monotone function of the Kullback–Leibler divergence $D_{\text{KL}}(P_{\text{post}} \| P_{\text{prior}})$.

Proof. Any measure satisfying these axioms is a function $\rho(P_{\text{post}}, P_{\text{prior}})$. Condition (ii) (invariance under reparameterization) implies $\rho$ depends on the distributions only through their relative densities, i.e., $\rho$ is a functional of the Radon–Nikodym derivative $dP_{\text{post}}/dP_{\text{prior}}$. Thus we can write $\rho(P,Q) = f\left( \int g\left( \frac{dP}{dQ} \right) dQ \right)$ for some functions $f,g$.

Condition (iii) (additivity over independent data) is the chain rule property. Consider two observations $x_1,x_2$ that are conditionally independent. The posterior after $x_1$ is $P_1$, and after $x_2$ further is $P_{12}$. The additive property requires

\rho(P_{12}, P_0) = \rho(P_1, P_0) + \rho(P_{12}, P_1).

This implies a functional equation. For probability densities with respect to a common measure, let $p_0$ be prior, $p_1$ posterior after $x_1$, $p_2$ posterior after $x_2$ given $p_1$. The densities satisfy $p_1(x) = p_0(x) L_1(x)/Z_1$, $p_2(x) = p_1(x) L_2(x)/Z_2$ where $L_i$ are likelihoods. The combined update $p_{12} = p_0 L_1 L_2 / Z_{12}$. The surprise functional $\rho$ must satisfy $\rho(p_{12},p_0) = \rho(p_1,p_0) + \rho(p_{12},p_1)$. As shown by Bernardo (1979) and later by Itti and Baldi (2009), the unique (up to a multiplicative constant) additive information measure that also satisfies the normalization $\rho(p,p)=0$ is the KL divergence. Specifically, any functional of the form $\int \phi(p_1/p_0) p_0$ that is additive must have $\phi(u) = c (u \log u - u + 1)$ or an affine transformation thereof. The requirement $\rho(P,Q)=0$ iff $P=Q$ fixes the constant $c>0$. Hence $\rho$ is proportional to $D_{\text{KL}}(P_{\text{post}} \| P_{\text{prior}})$. ∎

B.2 Derivation of Local and Global Surprise Measures

Local surprise (Definition 2.12): For indicator $O_{i,t}$ with parent latent node $H_j$, the local surprise quantifies how much the observation changes the belief about that parent. After observing $O_{i,t}$, the posterior over $H_j$ becomes $P(H_j \mid O_{i,t}, \mathbf{O}_{1:t-1})$. The prior in this local update is the predictive distribution $P(H_j \mid \mathbf{O}_{1:t-1})$. The KL divergence between these two captures the information gain. In the PIO, this is computed directly as part of the Bayesian update: when the particle filter weights are updated, the effective sample size change is related to the KL. For computational efficiency, one can approximate the local surprise as the log-ratio of posterior to prior probabilities at the mode, or use the analytical formula for conjugate models.

Global novelty index (Definition 2.13): The day’s information stream is embedded into a vector $\mathbf{E}_t$ using a pre-trained sentence encoder. We maintain a rolling Gaussian process (or VAE) predictive distribution $P(\mathbf{E}_t \mid \mathbf{E}_{1:t-1})$. The novelty index is defined as $D_{\text{KL}}(\hat{P}(\mathbf{E}_t \mid \mathbf{E}_{1:t-1}) \| P_{\text{ref}}(\mathbf{E}))$, but in practice we use the negative log-likelihood of $\mathbf{E}_t$ under the predictive model, which is proportional to the KL if the predictive model is the posterior and the reference is the prior. Specifically, if the predictive model is a Gaussian with mean $\mu_t$ and covariance $\Sigma_t$, then $-\log p(\mathbf{E}_t) \propto (\mathbf{E}_t - \mu_t)^\top \Sigma_t^{-1}(\mathbf{E}_t - \mu_t)$. This serves as an anomaly score.

B.3 Embedding the Unknown-Unknown Node (Proof of Proposition 2.3)

Proposition 2.3 states that for any fixed $\mathbf{H}_t$, the probability $P(S_d \mid \mathbf{H}_t, U_t)$ is strictly monotone increasing in $U_t$. The structural equation is

\operatorname{logit} P(S_d \mid \mathbf{H}_t, U_t) = \operatorname{logit} P(S_d \mid \mathbf{H}_t) + \lambda U_t,

with $\lambda > 0$. Since the logit function $f(x) = \log(x/(1-x))$ is strictly increasing, its inverse (the logistic function) is also strictly increasing. For any $U_t > 0$, the argument to the logistic function increases by $\lambda U_t$, which raises the probability. Formally, let $p_0 = P(S_d \mid \mathbf{H}_t)$. Then

P(S_d \mid \mathbf{H}_t, U_t) = \frac{1}{1 + \exp(-\operatorname{logit}(p_0) - \lambda U_t)}.

Taking derivative with respect to $U_t$ yields

\frac{\partial}{\partial U_t} P(S_d \mid \mathbf{H}_t, U_t) = \lambda \cdot p (1-p) > 0

for $0 < p < 1$, which is guaranteed. Hence monotonicity holds strictly. ∎

