Appendix D Second-Order Uncertainty and Epistemic Decomposition

D.1 Proof of Variance Decomposition (Theorem 2.2)

The law of total variance (or Eve’s law) states: for any random variable $X$ and conditioning variable $Z$,

\operatorname{Var}(X) = \mathbb{E}_Z[\operatorname{Var}(X \mid Z)] + \operatorname{Var}_Z(\mathbb{E}[X \mid Z]).

Set $X = P(S_d \mid \mathcal{D})$ as the posterior probability (which is a random variable due to uncertainty in the model). Take $Z$ to be the DAG structure $\mathcal{G}$. Then the decomposition follows directly. This decomposition is exact and requires no approximation.

Interpretation:

· $\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{G}}[\operatorname{Var}(P(S_d \mid \mathcal{D}, \mathcal{G}))]$ is the average (over plausible graphs) of the posterior variance of the risk given a fixed graph. This variance arises from parameter uncertainty (aleatoric with respect to the graph).
· $\operatorname{Var}_{\mathcal{G}}[\mathbb{E}(P(S_d \mid \mathcal{D}, \mathcal{G}))]$ measures how much the conditional expectation varies across different plausible graphs; it is the structural uncertainty, which is reducible if the true graph were identified.

The same decomposition can be applied to other sources of uncertainty, e.g., reliability parameters.

D.2 Hierarchical Beta-Dirichlet Specification and Moment Matching

For a binary risk node $S_d$, we model $p_d \sim \text{Beta}(\alpha_d, \beta_d)$. The hyperparameters $(\alpha_d,\beta_d)$ are updated using data through a Beta-Binomial conjugate analysis if observed binary outcomes become available. For the prior, the IDEA protocol elicits a best estimate $\hat{p}$ and a 90% credible interval $[\underline{p},\overline{p}]$ for the probability. We map these to $(\alpha,\beta)$ by solving:

\frac{\alpha}{\alpha+\beta} = \hat{p}, \qquad \text{and} \qquad \overline{p} - \underline{p} \approx 2 \, z_{0.05} \sqrt{ \frac{\hat{p}(1-\hat{p})}{\alpha+\beta+1} }.

From the interval, we compute an effective sample size $\nu = \alpha+\beta$. Using the normal approximation to the Beta distribution, the half-width of the 90% interval is $z_{0.05} \sqrt{ \frac{\hat{p}(1-\hat{p})}{\nu+1} }$. Thus

\nu = \frac{\hat{p}(1-\hat{p})}{ ((\overline{p}-\underline{p})/(2 z_{0.05}))^2 } - 1.

Then $\alpha = \hat{p} \nu$, $\beta = (1-\hat{p}) \nu$. This prior is used as the initial Beta distribution. When new domain observations arrive, the Beta can be updated by treating the effective sample size as pseudo-counts. For multi-class nodes, we use a Dirichlet prior with analogous moment matching.

D.3 Uncertainty Reporting and Constraint Score

The PIO uncertainty report (Definition 2.18) consists of posterior mean, 50% and 90% credible intervals, and the aleatoric/epistemic components. The constraint score $\xi_d = 1 - (\text{CI}_{90\%\text{-width}})/\hat{p}_d$ (capped at 0 and 1) measures relative precision; a value near 0 indicates very wide interval relative to the estimate, reflecting deep ignorance. The score aids in quickly assessing which risk estimates are well-constrained by data.

