Appendix F Posterior Contraction Theorem (Proof of Theorem 2.3)

We provide a rigorous sketch, expanding the main text.

Theorem 2.3. Under assumptions (a)–(d), for any $\delta > 0$,

P(\mathcal{G}^*, \|\Theta - \Theta^*\| \le \delta \mid \mathbf{O}_{1:T}) \xrightarrow{\mathbb{P}} 1 \quad \text{as } T \to \infty.

Assumptions:

· (a) The true DAG $\mathcal{G}^*$ belongs to the support of the prior $P(\mathcal{G})$.
· (b) For each indicator $i$, contamination fraction $\varepsilon_i < 1/2$ (so the clean model dominates).
· (c) Indicators are asymptotically informative: for each $i$, the mutual information $I(H_{\text{pa}(i)}; O_i) > 0$ under the true model.
· (d) The prior over continuous parameters $\Theta$ (CPT entries, etc.) is proper and has full support around $\Theta^*$.

Proof Outline. We prove contraction in three stages.

1. Structure posterior contraction. The marginal likelihood $P(\mathcal{O}_{1:T} \mid \mathcal{G})$ is used to compare DAGs. Under condition (c) and with $T$ large, the Bayes factor between $\mathcal{G}^*$ and any misspecified $\mathcal{G}$ (which implies a different factorization) converges to zero exponentially fast. Because the true data-generating process is $\mathcal{G}^*$ with parameters $\Theta^*$, and the contaminated observations still retain sufficient signal (by condition (b)), the log marginal likelihood ratio $\frac{1}{T} \log \frac{P(\mathcal{O}_{1:T} \mid \mathcal{G}^*)}{P(\mathcal{O}_{1:T} \mid \mathcal{G})} \to \text{KL}(P^* \| P_{\mathcal{G}})$ where the limit is positive for misspecified $\mathcal{G}$ under standard identifiability conditions. Hence $P(\mathcal{G} \neq \mathcal{G}^* \mid \mathcal{O}_{1:T}) \to 0$ exponentially, so the posterior concentrates on $\mathcal{G}^*$.

2. Reliability parameter contraction. As shown in Proposition 2.2, the online reliability estimator converges to the true confusion matrices $\mathbf{\Pi}^*$. For finite $T$, the posterior over reliability parameters contracts around the true values at a $\sqrt{T}$ rate. Coupled with the structure contraction, we can condition on $\mathcal{G}^*$ and then apply Bernstein–von Mises theorem for the parameters.

3. Parametric contraction conditional on graph. Given the true DAG and consistent reliability estimates, the remaining parameters are the CPT entries. The model is a Bayesian network with Dirichlet priors on discrete nodes; standard posterior concentration results (Ghosal and van der Vaart, 2017) yield that the posterior of $\Theta$ concentrates in a neighborhood of the true parameter $\Theta^*$. The contamination is handled by the robust likelihood: asymptotically, the contamination fraction estimates converge to the true $\varepsilon_i^*$, and the MMD-robust posterior is consistent. Therefore, the joint posterior concentrates as claimed. ∎

Note on contamination: Under Huber’s model, the clean data component proportion $1-\varepsilon_i$ remains positive; the robust likelihood downweights outliers, so the bulk of the data drives the contraction. As $T \to \infty$, the effect of contamination is diluted because the number of clean observations grows linearly while the contamination points may be arbitrary but cannot dominate the KL divergence if $\varepsilon_i<1/2$. Formalizing this requires standard robust Bayesian asymptotics (e.g., Alquier and Gerber, 2024).

