Appendix G Counterfactual Query Interface and Do-Calculus

G.1 Definition of Interventions

A counterfactual query is specified by a set of indicators $I$ and hypothetical values $\mathbf{o}^* = \{o_i^*\}_{i\in I}$. The query asks: what would the risk be had we observed $\mathbf{o}^*$ at time $t+1$, given the actual history $\mathbf{O}_{1:t}$? In causal terms, this is an intervention $\operatorname{do}(O_{i,t+1} = o_i^*)_{i\in I}$, which removes the incoming edges from the parents of those observation nodes. The modified model has the same structure except that for $i\in I$, the observation is fixed, not generated by $P(O_i \mid \operatorname{pa}(O_i))$. The posterior over the latent states is then computed using this interventional model.

G.2 Computation via Modified Bayesian Update

Algorithmically, to answer a counterfactual:

1. Start with the filtering posterior at time $t$: $P(\mathbf{H}_t \mid \mathbf{O}_{1:t})$.
2. Advance the state via the transition model to get $P(\mathbf{H}_{t+1} \mid \mathbf{O}_{1:t})$.
3. Perform a Bayesian update using the hypothetical observations $o_i^*$ for $i\in I$, but treating them as deterministic evidence, i.e., multiply by the indicator $\mathbf{1}[O_i = o_i^*]$ and renormalize. For indicators not in $I$, use the actual likelihood as usual, though there are no actual observations at time $t+1$ yet; alternatively, we can simply fix the values and omit the likelihood for non-intervened indicators. The formal do-calculus tells us that conditioning on an intervention breaks the parent links; the effect is that the contribution to the posterior is the product of indicator functions, which is equivalent to setting the observation likelihood to a point mass. The resulting posterior over $\mathbf{H}_{t+1}$ is the counterfactual distribution.
4. Marginalize to obtain $P_{\text{cf}}(S_d \mid \mathbf{O}_{1:t}, \mathbf{o}^*)$.

In the PIO, this is implemented by temporarily overriding the observation model for the intervened indicators. The structure-averaged approach then uses the same model averaging across DAG samples, each of which has the appropriate interventional graph. This yields a coherent counterfactual probability that can be used for scenario planning.



